he method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Number","success_criteria":["I can use mixed review with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify choose strategy and explain why it matters.","I can practise brackets independently on a new prompt.","I can notice communicate needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Index laws review mixes expansion, laws and notation traps with negatives and powers of ten.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working","Index laws review mixes expansion, laws and notation traps with negatives and powers of ten"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about mixed review with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSimplify (10^2)^3 ÷ 10^3 = 10^6 ÷ 10^3 = 10^3 = 1000. Flag: (−5)^2 vs −5^2.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSimplify and evaluate two of: 3^2×3^3, (10^3)^2, −4^2; explain bracket choice on the last.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: 3^5=243; 10^6=1_000_000; −16 with explanation.","explain":"Index laws review mixes expansion, laws and notation traps with negatives and powers of ten. Communicate steps so another student can follow; box final answers with units if contextual.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Blurring laws when tired—write the law name above each step. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Mixed review.","caption":"Communicate clearly.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Law name</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">above the step</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Bracket flag</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">negatives</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Simplify (10^2)^3 ÷ 10^3 = 10^6 ÷ 10^3 = 10^3 = 1000. Flag: (−5)^2 vs −5^2. Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Simplify (10^2)^3 ÷ 10^3 = 10^6 ÷ 10^3 = 10^3 = 1000. Flag: (−5)^2 vs −5^2. Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working","Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about mixed review with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSimplify (10^2)^3 ÷ 10^3 = 10^6 ÷ 10^3 = 10^3 = 1000. Flag: (−5)^2 vs −5^2. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY 